INT. TREEHOLLOW ARCHIVE OF THE MOSS-KEEPERS - TWILIGHT

Inside the trunk of a tree so old its heartwood has hollowed into a cathedral. Shelves of bark scrolls spiral up the walls. Fireflies drift among them like patient candles.

At a root-table in the center sit four figures, each with a small lamp.

HESPERIA THORNQUILL (60s, keeper-in-chief, silver braid to her knees) turns the pages of a mossbound book. She is calm in the way of those who have never been hurried.

Beside her, the young scribe OSWIN FERNLEIGH (17, ink to the elbows) copies a line, tongue between his teeth.

Across from them a travelling tinker, BRAMBLE-JACK (40s, patched coat, pockets that clink), polishes a brass key with his sleeve.

At the end of the table, a stranger from the river-folk, ILUNA WAVEREAD (30s, damp hair, a shell necklace), sits with her chin in her palm.

HESPERIA
(to the table)
We begin the evening readings.

Nobody moves. The fireflies drift.

OSWIN
Keeper Thornquill, should I record the weather?

HESPERIA
Yes, child. Rain in the east, none in the west.

OSWIN
Rain in the east.

He writes. She smiles at the book.

BRAMBLE-JACK
(low, to himself)
Same rain as yesterday. Same rain as a hundred years.

HESPERIA
Bramble-Jack, if you have a thought, share it with us all.

BRAMBLE-JACK
Nothing, Keeper. Just brass.

He holds the key up. It catches a firefly's light.

ILUNA
It's a lovely key.

BRAMBLE-JACK
Isn't it? Found it in a trout.

ILUNA
In a trout?

BRAMBLE-JACK
Don't ask.

She laughs, soft, and he looks pleased.

Hesperia closes the book. Opens another.

HESPERIA
Iluna Wavereads. You came with a question.

ILUNA
I did. Though it's been so peaceful, I almost forgot.

HESPERIA
Ask it anyway.

ILUNA
(to the room)
The river-folk say a door lies under this tree. They say it opens once a generation.

OSWIN
(whispering)
The cellar door.

HESPERIA
Oswin.

OSWIN
Sorry, Keeper.

Hesperia turns a page. The parchment rustles.

HESPERIA
There is a door. It does not open for questions.

ILUNA
What does it open for?

HESPERIA
Patience.

BRAMBLE-JACK
She's been waiting six years, Keeper. I counted.

HESPERIA
Then six years is not yet enough.

Iluna sighs, amused rather than defeated.

ILUNA
Oswin, would you like to hear what the river says about the archive?

OSWIN
Please.

ILUNA
It says you keep the quietest place in the whole forest.

OSWIN
It does?

ILUNA
It does. And it says the quiet is the door.

Hesperia looks up from the book for the first time. Studies the river-woman a long moment.

HESPERIA
Who taught you that?

ILUNA
Nobody. I listened.

HESPERIA
(to herself)
Well. Well, well.

She closes the book on her finger.

HESPERIA (CONT'D)
Bramble-Jack, bring the key.

He rises, key in his palm, and crosses the root floor. At the base of the spiral stair, he kneels. The key fits nothing visible.

BRAMBLE-JACK
Where do I--

HESPERIA
Wherever it is quietest.

He closes his eyes. Listens. Then touches the key to the grain of the wood, and the grain opens, gently, like an eyelid.

Warm gold light spills across the floor.

OSWIN
(to Iluna)
You did that.

ILUNA
We all did.

The fireflies drift toward the light, in no hurry at all.
